o be the last observed point a n a_{n} . Equivalently, a n = H θ ​ ( r n ∣ p ) − I θ ​ ( r n ; r 1 , … , r n − 1 ∣ p ) a_{n}=H_{	heta}(r_{n}\mid p)-I_{	heta}(r_{n};r_{1},\ldots,r_{n-1}\mid p) : the response’s individual surprise under θ 	heta minus how much the prior responses make it predictable. High a n a_{n} therefore requires r n r_{n} to be both individually surprising and not made predictable by the others, two properties one would want a diversity score to reward. No fitting or extrapolation step is involved. The curve a k a_{k} is itself informative: its shape distinguishes a one-mode collapse (sharp drop to a low floor) from richer diversity (gradual decline to a higher floor) in ways the endpoint alone obscures. 3.2 The Coherence Term The curve alone is fooled by pure noise: θ 	heta can never predict random tokens from one another, so a n a_{n} stays at the high unconditional surprise level and noise would dominate the metric. The distinguishing signal lies in how plausible θ 	heta finds each individual response, independent of the others. Let h θ ​ ( r ∣ p ) = − 1 ‖ r ‖ ​ log 2 ⁡ θ ⁡ ( r ∣ p ) h_{	heta}(r\mid p)=- rac{1}{\|r\|}\log_{2}	heta(r\mid p) be the per-byte cross-entropy of response r r under θ 	heta conditioned on the prompt alone. Define coherence as the geometric mean of the per-byte probabilities: C = 2 − 1 n ∑ i = 1 n h θ ( r i ∣ p ) = 1 PPL θ ​ ( π , p ) , C=2^{- rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}h_{	heta}(r_{i}\mid p)}= rac{1}{\mathrm{PPL}_{	heta}(\pi,p)}, (4) the reciprocal of the geometric-mean per-byte perplexity 3 3 3 In our Tevet evaluation with Qwen2.5-3B, the central 90% (5th to 95th percentile) of per-response bits-per-byte values across 48705 responses span [ 0.65 , 2.20 ] [0.65,2.20] , giving C ∈ [ 0.22 , 0.64 ] C\in[0.22,0.64] ; the mean per-set C C across 9741 response sets is 0.42. that θ 	heta assigns to the responses individually. 4 4 4 For scale, the bits/byte score is the average per-byte cross-entropy ℓ ¯  ar{\ell} , related to cohe